A||||6||Cooter||Which tire sizes?||||5||
Z||000000||Cooter||12-31-1999||06:56 PM||||My 88 GT is in bad need of some new tires. It has 225-60/15's on the front and 235-60/15's on the back. I know that because the 88's have different size wheels front/back but I think the current tires are too big. What sizes would be best?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||12-31-1999||07:58 PM||||I can't say what would be best in sizing, but stock was 205/60/15 front, and 215/60/15 rear.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cobra427||12-31-1999||08:48 PM||||cooter, what are you looking for in the ride of your car? Stock or do you want to up grade in the handling dept. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||12-31-1999||09:56 PM||||I am upgrading the suspension with poly bushings and better shocks/struts, so better handling is what I would like to have, but not at the expense of fuel economy due to too large of a tire or tires scrubbing when turning or traveling on bumpy roads. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||cobra427||01-01-2000||12:09 AM||||cooter this is one possibility that you can do to make your car handle better as I did to my 86 GT.I put 1" sway bar on the front and a 7/8" on the rear. I went to the Koenig GT-R 16" wheels with 215/50/16 front and 225/50/16 on the rears. The tires were BF Goodrich Comp T/A's. The difference in tire size was minimal.Diameter difference between the two was 1.1" Circumference difference was .94";and revs/mile was a difference of 10 revolutions more, All mesurements represent a smaller tire except for the revs. The speedo error is only a livable 1.18%.I find that the lower profile tires make the most difference. My next moves will be to put tubular arms all around with poly bushings and dampen everything with Koni's. Would love to do the Ryan slalum front end kit. Out of my range right now. But this works well for me. Just a thought.... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||01-01-2000||12:55 AM||||Coot,<P>Do the upgrades you have planed. Adding polly to the front sway commonly has the same result as a bigger sway. It's cheapper too.<P>What tires to use depends on what rims you have. and half a hundred other things. You'd be suprised what I get out of my 87SC with it's 195/70R14's.<P>As I've said before...... check the tire maker specs. you want tires within 1/2 of the "test rim size" on your rims. For example. If a tire is 195/70R14 when it's tested on a 6" wide rim you want your rim to be within 5.5-6.5 inches wide. <P>The tire may be safely mounted on any rim in the maufacturers width limit but Ideal is to stay very close to test rim size.<P>This "test rim size" is very important. It's the rim that the tires size was calculated on. If you mount the wheel on a diferant width rim you change the tire size.<P>You also want to look up the tread width, Never use tire size as a tread width measure. tread width can vary over 1" for any given tire size. Again for example, on a 195/70R14 tire the tread width can run from about 5.5 - 6.5 inches.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cobra427||01-01-2000||01:55 AM||||i should have also stated that the rims were 7" wide. Also the backspace was approximately3/8" closer to the hub. Not a problem tho. Nothing hits or scrapes anywhere, front or rear.||||reg||1
